{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2125","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2125","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2125\",\"parent_revision\":\"36250340f418077b69830d9a8e9bdb014cf1937397d022d6ece1c17ff89e870f\",\"root_revision\":\"36250340f418077b69830d9a8e9bdb014cf1937397d022d6ece1c17ff89e870f\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nWhen multiple skill descriptions match a user request, Claude has no tie-breaker signal. Routing decisions become probabilistic rather than deterministic. Users get the wrong skill activated, or the orchestrator must read multiple SKILL.md bodies to disambiguate \u2014 wasting context tokens that explicit negative scope in the frontmatter would save.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: Pattern observed in research/skill-generation-tools/waza.md \u2014 Waza requires every skill description to end with a `Not for: \u2026` clause (verified in `.worktrees/waza/skills/check/SKILL.md` line 3 and `.worktrees/waza/AGENTS.md` Adding Or Changing A Skill section). The exclusion clause acts as a tie-breaker and reduces routing ambiguity.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer reading the documentation**, I want to **skill descriptions lack explicit \\\"not for\\\" exclusion clause for routing disambiguation** so that **documentation is accurate and trustworthy**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Agent task - auto-derived from research/skill-generation-tools/waza.md\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-04\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nSkill descriptions in this repo list positive triggers only \u2014 what the skill is FOR \u2014 with no explicit clause naming what the skill is NOT for. With ~280 skills across 30 plugins and many overlapping triggers, Claude has no explicit signal to disambiguate when multiple descriptions could plausibly match the same user request.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Where Observed\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/skill-creator/SKILL.md` Step 5 \u00a7\\\"Frontmatter\\\" (lines ~471-477) instructs authors to \\\"Include both what the Skill does and specific triggers/contexts for when to use it. Include all 'when to use' information here.\\\" \u2014 no instruction to include negative scope.\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/write-frontmatter-description/SKILL.md` \u2014 frontmatter description authoring guidance does not require a `Not for` clause.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/discovery/SKILL.md` line 3 \u2014 example of a description that lists positive triggers only.\\n- Skills with overlapping triggers exist (e.g. `discovery` vs `find-cause` vs `rt-ica`); routing relies on description matching alone.\\n- `skilllint` validators do not flag missing exclusion clauses.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"c4b0a4485f4ce932b35cc446b1b05d1e38e6e9139a76b2e65256069b6587c9f9\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCcLdA\"}"}